   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law and the penal law, in relation to disqualification of employment for certain criminal history information and deeming an individual with a developmental disability is incapable of giving consent",
    "basePrintNo": "S5711",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5711-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §13.40, Ment Hyg L; amd §130.05, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5711\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the mental hygiene law and the penal law, in relation to\ndisqualification of employment for certain criminal history information\nand deeming an individual with a developmental disability is incapable\nof giving consent\n\nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would: (1) prohibit the Office for People With Developmental\nDisabilities (OPWDD) and authorized agencies from hiring individuals\nconvicted of certain violent and serious crimes; and (2) provide that\nan individual with a developmental disability who is receiving\nservices from OPWDD or an agency operated, certified, authorized or\nfunded through OPWDD is incapable of consenting to sexual contact\nwith an employee, intern, consultant, contractor or volunteer of such\noffice or agency where the individual receives services.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend the Mental Hygiene Law (MHL) to add\na new section 13.40 to provide that OPWDD and authorized agencies may\nnot hire an individual who has been convicted of certain crimes,\nincluding homicide, sex offenses, felony level assaults, endangering\nthe welfare of a child, incompetent of physically disabled person,\nkidnapping, robbery, any attempt to commit one of the specified\ncrimes, or any comparable offense in any other jurisdiction.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend Penal Law § 130.05(3) to add a new\nparagraph (i) to provide that, for purposes of determining whether an\nindividual can consent to sexual contact for purposes of criminal\nliability under Article 130 of the Penal Law, an individual with a\ndevelopmental disability cannot consent if he or she is receiving\nservices from OPWDD or an agency operated, certified, authorized or\nfunded through OPWDD and the actor is an employee, intern,\nconsultant, contractor or volunteer of such agency where the\nindividual receives services.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would provide for an immediate effective date.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nCivil Services Law § 50 provides authorization for criminal background\nchecks for prospective State employees. Labor Law . 201-a further\nauthorizes fingerprinting of State employees. MHL § 16.3 3 authorizes\ncriminal background checks for prospective operators, volunteers and\nemployees of service providers that contract with, are approved by,\nor are otherwise authorized to provide services, provided that the\nperson \"will have regular and substantial unsupervised or unrestricted\nphysical contact with the clients of such provider.\"\n\nExecutive Law § 845-b sets forth the procedures for the conduct of\ncriminal background checks and the rights and obligations of the\n\n\nparties involved, including State agencies that conduct the\nbackground checks, employing private sector agencies and the\nindividuals who are fingerprinted. Section 845-b provides that where\nthe criminal history information for a subject reveals: (1) a\nconviction at any time for a sex offense; (2) a felony conviction\nwithin the past ten years involving violence; or (3) a conviction for\nendangering the welfare of an incompetent or physically disabled\nperson pursuant to section 260.25 of the Penal Law, or any comparable\noffense in any 'other jurisdiction, the fingerprinting agency (OPWDD)\nshall deny employment, disapprove the application or direct the\nemploying agency to do so unless it determines, in its discretion,\nthat approval will not in any way jeopardize the health, safety or\nwelfare of the beneficiaries of the services.\n\nArticle 23-A of the Correction Law sets forth the standard for\nevaluation of criminal history information. Correction Law § 752\nprovides that employment or licensure may not be denied to an\napplicant by reason of prior criminal conviction(s) unless: (1) there\nis a direct relationship between one or more of the criminal offenses\nand the specific license or employment sought, or (2) the granting of\nemployment/license would involve an unreasonable risk to property or\nto the safety or welfare of specific individuals or the general\npublic. Correction Law § 753(1) mandates that review of the criminal\nhistory record be based on consideration of nine statutory criteria,\nincluding, among other factors, the time elapsed since the offense,\nthe individual's age at the time of the occurrence, the seriousness\nof the offense, the specific duties which the individual will\nperform, and \"[t]he public policy of the state... to encourage the\nlicensure and employment of persons previously convicted of one or\nmore criminal offenses.\"\n\nArticle 130 of the Penal Law governs criminal liability for sex\noffenses.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nProtecting the safety and welfare of individuals receiving services in\nthe OPWDD system must be the highest priority. This bill would\nheighten protections for individuals receiving services in the OPWDD\nsystem and ensure that individuals employed in the system are of the\nhighest caliber by prohibiting the employment of persons who have\nbeen convicted of certain violent and serious crimes. While most\ncriminal convictions would be evaluated under the standards set forth\nin the Executive Law and the Corrections Law, certain individuals who\nhave committed the most heinous of crimes would be ineligible for\nemployment. This would include convictions for offenses such as\nhomicides, sex crimes, arsons, and sexual performance by a child, and\nfelony level assaults, robberies, kidnappings and endangering the\nwelfare of a child, vulnerable elderly person or incompetent person.\nAll other criminal convictions will continue to be reviewed in\naccordance with the standard set forth in Correction Law Article 23-A\nand Executive Law § 845-b.\n\n\nThis bill would also enhance the safety of individuals who receive\nservices from OPWDD or authorized agencies by making it clear that\nsuch individuals are legally incapable of consenting to sexual\ncontact with employees or other similar individuals who provide\nservices to them. Under Penal Law § 130.05(1), lack of consent is an\nelement of all sex offenses defined in Penal Law Article 130.\nRecognizing that consent is not-truly voluntary when a perpetrator is\nin a special position of authority over a victim, the Penal Law\n§130.05(3) thereafter provides that certain individuals are legally\nincapable of consenting to sexual contact, including certain .\nindividuals who are incarcerated and in the custody of the Department\nof Corrections and Community Supervision (DOCCS), or in the care of\nthe Office of Children and Family Services (OCFS). There is no\nsimilar provision that an individual with a developmental disability\nwho is receiving services in the OPWDD system is incapable of\nconsenting to sexual contact with employees of OPWDD or the agency in\nwhich they receive services, even though these employees are in the\nsame special positions of authority as DOCCS or OCFS employees.\n\nWhile such contact is considered sexual abuse under OPWDD regulations,\nthere is no presumption in the Penal Law that such contact is\ncriminal. Making this amendment will send a clear message to\nemployees, interns, consultants, contractors or volunteers of such\nagencies that engaging in sexual contact with individuals who are\nreceiving services will not be tolerated.\n\nIt also will ensure that such behavior will be effectively punished. A\nreview of cases involving sexual assaults by employees against\nconsumers receiving services in the OPWDD system has shown that in a\nmajority of cases law enforcement officials have been unable to\nprosecute alleged offenders. Generally, law enforcement took action\nonly when there were eyewitnesses to the crime or physical evidence\nsubstantiating the use of force. In addition, in many cases,\nconsumers initially denied the abuse out of fear that something would\nhappen to the person on whom they relied for their daily care. This\nbill would appropriately give prosecutors the ability to establish\nlack of consent based on the perpetrators' relationship of authority\nover the consumer, just as is done for similarly situated victims in\nthe OCFS and DOCCS systems.\nAs a result, individuals receiving services in the OPWDD system would\nreceive the highest level of protection from sexual advances by\nindividuals employed by OPWDD or authorized agencies, and staff who\nengage in this criminal behavior would be appropriately subject to\ncriminal penalties.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill would have no fiscal implications.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis bill would take effect immediately.\n\n",
